nreg [44:0] tmp3; //21 + 16 =37\n```\ntmp1: 21+24=45 ✓. tmp2: 21+35=56 ✓. tmp3 comment says \"21+16=37\" but the reg is 45 bits — the comment is inconsistent (it would be 21+24=45 to hold the full product; the \"16\" is the comment's error referencing the claimed 16-bit sin/cos). The actual declared width [44:0] = 45 bits is correct for 21+24. Good, I can point this out as a comment inconsistency.\n\nThen:\n```\nx =($signed(k_inv0) * $signed(x_)) + ($signed(k_inv1) * $signed(y_)) + ($signed(k_inv2) * $signed(z_));  //comment: 位宽 21+35=56, 小数位 8+22=30\n```\nWait the comment says \"x的位宽为 21+35=56位，但小数位为 8+22=30位\" — but that's wrong-ish. Let me read it. The comment is garbled (encoding issues) but says: bit width 21+35=56, fraction bits 8+22=30. Hmm.\n\nActually the sum for x: k_inv0*x_ (21+24=45) + k_inv1*y_ (21+35=56) + k_inv2*z_ (21+24=45). The widest term is 56 bits. So x needs 56 bits + extra for the sum of 3 terms. The reg x is declared [55:0] = 56 bits. So they sized it to the widest term (56) without extra carry bits — acceptable since values are bounded.\n\nThe comment about fraction bits: x_ has 14 fraction bits (claimed), y_ has 22 fraction bits (v is Q13.22, 22 fraction), z_ has 14 fraction bits. k_inv has 8 fraction bits. So:\n- k_inv0*x_: fraction = 8+14 = 22 bits\n- k_inv1*y_: fraction = 8+22 = 30 bits\n- k_inv2*z_: fraction = 8+14 = 22 bits\n\nThe terms have DIFFERENT fraction alignments! This is a real issue. To add them, you must align the binary points. The widest is 30 fraction bits (from k_inv1*y_, but k_inv1=0 so that term is 0). The comment says \"小数位为8+22=30\" referencing the y_ term.\n\nThis is interesting for the lecture: the three products have misaligned binary points, which is a fixed-point design subtlety/risk. I should explain this.\n\nThen the floor/ceil extraction:\n```\nweight_y00 = y[55:30]; //floor\nweight_x00 = x[55:30]; //floor\n```\ny is 56 bits. y[55:30] takes the top 26 bits. y's fraction is 30 bits (per comment, from the y_ pa