8 (1 sign + 12 integer + 8 fraction), derive k_inv0=2707.47, k_inv2=550.5, k_inv8=1.\n3. Bit-width expansion in multiplication: 21-bit × 24-bit = 45-bit, 21-bit × 35-bit = 56-bit.\n4. The three-term matrix-vector multiply, sizing to widest term (56 bits).\n5. Binary point alignment subtlety: terms have different fraction widths; comment says 30 fraction bits for the result.\n6. floor = high-bit truncation y[55:30] (drop 30 fraction bits), ceil = floor+1.\n7. Overflow & timing risks.\n\nFor the fraction alignment issue, I'll explain it carefully but not overclaim a specific bug — I'll note that the result is treated as Q(56-30).30 = Q26.30 and the lower 30 bits are dropped for floor, and that this is consistent with the comment. Whether the sin/cos operand's actual fraction width perfectly matches is a CORDIC-config-dependent detail (marked 待确认 for exact CORDIC Q format), so I'll present the intended design and flag the risk.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The cleaner interpretation: All products are sign-extended/aligned to the common 56-bit accumulator with 30 fraction bits. The y_ term (v) naturally has 30 fraction bits when... no. Let me reconsider y_'s format. v = dst_tl_y * coe = 11-bit integer × Q2.22 = Q13.22 (35 bits, 22 fraction). So y_ = v has 22 fraction bits. Then k_inv4 * y_ = Q13.8 × Q13.22 → fraction 8+22 = 30. So tmp2 (the y term) has 30 fraction bits. ✓ That's where \"30\" comes from.\n\nFor the x and z terms: k_inv0 * x_. If x_ (sin) has 14 fraction bits (per comment), then k_inv0*x_ = 8+14 = 22 fraction bits. To add to the y term (30 fraction), x_ term must be shifted left by 8 to align. But the code just adds them directly without shifting. \n\nHmm, but k_inv1 = 0, so the y-term in the `x` equation is 0. So x = k_inv0*x_ + k_inv2*z_ (both 22 fraction). And the code takes x[55:30], interpreting x as having 30 fraction bits. If x actually has 22 fraction bits, then x[55:30] would drop 30 bits including 8 integer bits → wrong by factor 256.\n\nThis