th 2707.47), k_inv2 = k_inv5 (both 550.5). This is the symmetric K matrix with fx=fy and cx=cy. ✓\n\nGreat, my decode is solid.\n\nNow let me also verify coe once more and present it cleanly. coe = 1/scale.\n1/2707.47 = 0.0003693740...\nQ2.22: 0.0003693740 × 2^22 = 0.0003693740 × 4194304 = 1549.0 → 1549 = 0x60D = binary `0000_0000_0000_0110_0000_1101` (24 bits).\n\nLet me verify 0x60D in 24 bits: 0x00060D = 0000 0000 0000 0110 0000 1101. \nHmm, my earlier parse gave bits 13-16 = 0110 (6), bits 17-20=0, bits 21-24 = 1101 (D). In hex that's 0x00060D. And 0x60D = 6*256 + 0*16 + 13 = 1536+13 = 1549. ✓\n\nBut wait, the original literal groups were `0_0_0000_0000_0001_1000_0011_01`. Let me re-examine: group `0001` then `1000`. So after the leading zeros we have `0001 1000` = the bits near position 11-18. 0001 1000 = 0x18 = 24. Hmm, that doesn't match my 0x60D.\n\nLet me re-parse very carefully by writing the literal without underscores:\n`0_0_0000_0000_0001_1000_0011_01`\nRemove underscores: `0000000000011000001 101`? No — let me just concatenate the group strings exactly:\n- \"0\"\n- \"0\"  \n- \"0000\"\n- \"0000\"\n- \"0001\"\n- \"1000\"\n- \"0011\"\n- \"01\"\n\nConcatenation: \"0\"+\"0\"+\"0000\"+\"0000\"+\"0001\"+\"1000\"+\"0011\"+\"01\"\n= \"0000000000011000 001101\"\n\nLet me write it: \n0\n00\n0000\n0000 → so far: 0 0 0000 0000 = \"0000000000\" (10 chars)\n0001 → \"0000000000\" + \"0001\" = \"00000000000001\" (14 chars)\n1000 → + \"1000\" = \"000000000000011000\" (18 chars)\n0011 → + \"0011\" = \"0000000000000110000011\" (22 chars)\n01 → + \"01\" = \"0000000000000110000011 01\" = \"000000000000011000001101\" (24 chars)\n\nSo the 24-bit string is: `0000 0000 0000 1100 0001 101` \nLet me group into nibbles from left (24 bits = 6 nibbles):\n`0000` `0000` `0001` `1000` `0011` `01`... \n\nHmm, 6 nibbles would be 24 bits. Let me take the 24-char string: \"000000000000011000001101\"\nGroup into nibbles (4 bits each):\n- chars 1-4: \"0000\"\n- chars 5-8: \"0000\"\n- chars 